<Layout
  title="How Many Plants Per Room for Clean Air"
  description="The real math behind NASA's findings: plant count, room size, and what actually makes a measurable difference in indoor air quality."
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pt-12 pb-20">
    <nav class="text-xs text-stone-400 mb-8">
      <a href="/blog" class="hover:text-stone-600 transition-colors">Blog</a>
      <span class="mx-2">/</span>
      <span class="text-stone-600">How Many Plants Per Room</span>
    </nav>
    <header class="mb-10">
      <time datetime="2026-06-12" class="text-xs text-stone-400 mb-3 block">June 12, 2026</time>
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tighter leading-tight text-stone-900 mb-4">How Many Plants Do You Need to Clean a Room's Air?</h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-stone-500 leading-relaxed">The real math behind NASA's findings — plant count, room size, and what actually makes a measurable difference.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="prose prose-stone max-w-none">
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The most common question after discovering air-purifying plants is the practical one: how many do I actually need? The answer is more nuanced than a simple number, but there is a well-researched starting point.</p>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-8 mb-3">The NASA Baseline</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Dr. B.C. Wolverton, the lead NASA researcher, suggested <strong>one 6-8 inch plant per 100 square feet</strong> of space. This means a typical 150 sq ft bedroom needs about 1-2 plants, while a 300 sq ft living room benefits from 3-4 plants.</p>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why More Isn't Always Better</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Plants filter air through their leaves and root systems. In a naturally ventilated home (windows open, air circulating), a moderate number of well-placed plants is more effective than cramming dozens into one corner. Airflow is crucial — plants can only filter the air that reaches them.</p>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-8 mb-3">Plant Power by Room</h2>
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-stone-900 mt-6 mb-2">Bedroom (150 sq ft)</h3>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">1-2 Snake Plants or Peace Lilies are ideal for bedrooms. Both release oxygen at night and add gentle humidity, which can improve sleep quality.</p>
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-stone-900 mt-6 mb-2">Living Room (300 sq ft)</h3>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">3-4 mixed plants targeting different VOCs work best in larger living spaces. A Rubber Plant handles formaldehyde, a Bamboo Palm tackles benzene, and a Spider Plant provides general filtration.</p>
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-stone-900 mt-6 mb-2">Home Office (100 sq ft)</h3>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">1-2 compact plants like Aloe Vera or Pothos fit neatly on a desk or shelf while filtering the air in a smaller workspace.</p>
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-stone-900 mt-6 mb-2">Bathroom (80 sq ft)</h3>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">1 English Ivy or Boston Fern works well in bathrooms, where they help manage mold spores and thrive in higher humidity.</p>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Bottom Line</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Start with 1-2 plants in the rooms where you spend the most time and observe. Better air quality isn't just about plant count — it's about choosing the right plant for the right pollutant in the right room.</p>
    </div>
